While the spectra of SGRs 1900+14 and 1806-20 are relatively hard, with indices between 2.0 and 2.5, SGR 0526-66 is much softer with a spectral index of 3.5. Interestingly, the spectral index range of SGR 1627-41 (2.2-3.8) spans all other SGR sources observed at their quiescent states, although we do not see the blackbody (bb) component observed in SGR 1900+14. This is not unexpected, given that the bb contribution in the unabsorbed flux of 1900+14 is of the order of 20%; since the column density along the line of sight for SGR 1627-41 is almost 10 times higher, extreme absorption between 0.1 - 2.0 keV would hinder the detection of any bb component that contributes less than at least 50% of the (unabsorbed) flux.

